re.search(pattern, string)

Looks for the first occurrence.
Returns a match object if found.
Returns None if not found.

An entry like <re.Match object; span=(7, 13), match='Python'> is a Python Match object returned by the re module. It indicates that your regular expression search successfully found a pattern match inside your string.Rather than returning a simple text string or a boolean, Python returns this object packed with detailed metadata about the match.Breakdown of the Match Object Datare.Match object: The underlying type or class of the returned data.span=(7, 13): The exact index positions of the match inside your original string. It starts at index 7 and goes up to, but does not include, index 13.match='Python': The actual substring text that triggered the match.How to Extract the Data In Your CodeYou can easily extract these pieces of information using built-in methods provided by the Python re module:.group(): Extracts the matched text string ('Python')..span(): Extracts the start and end positions as a tuple ((7, 13))..start(): Extracts only the starting index (7)..end(): Extracts only the ending index (13).

